= Glow Lamp =

A ring of 8 addressable LEDs driven by an ESP32-C3. WiFi is provisioned through
a captive portal, the lamp is controlled over a REST API and over MQTT, and the
firmware updates itself from GitHub releases.

Source: [https://github.com/brennanMKE/GlowLamp brennanMKE/GlowLamp]

== Hardware ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Part !! Detail
|-
| Board || ESP32-C3 (<code>esp32-c3-devkitm-1</code>)
|-
| Ring || WS2812B, 8 LEDs
|-
| Data pin || GPIO 4
|-
| Power || 5 V, capped in firmware at 500 mA
|}

Wire the ring's data line to GPIO 4 and share ground between the ring's supply
and the ESP32-C3. At the default brightness of 64 the 8 LEDs are well inside the
500 mA cap, so USB power is fine; run it near 255 and use a separate 5 V supply.

=== The ring is one light ===

This is the constraint the firmware is built around. Behind a diffuser, several
colors an inch apart do not read as several colors &mdash; they mix into a muddy
white. So the ring shows '''one color at a time''', and the effects differ in how
that color changes over time rather than in where it sits on the ring.

''Flicker'' is the deliberate exception: it relies on that mixing to look like a
flame, and is meant to be given colors that are already close together.

== Deployed lamps ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Name !! Hostname !! Location
|-
| Castor || <code>castor-lamp.local</code> ||
|-
| Pollux || <code>pollux-lamp.local</code> ||
|}

Each lamp has two names. The '''name''' is free text ("Castor"), shown in the UI
and announced over mDNS, and changes take effect immediately. The '''hostname'''
is the mDNS label, lowercase letters, digits and hyphens; the lamp answers at
<code><hostname>.local</code> and needs a reboot to republish it.

Give every lamp a distinct hostname. Two lamps sharing one would both answer to
it, and nothing would report the conflict.

== Finding a lamp ==

Lamps announce <code>_glowlamp._tcp</code> on port 80 over mDNS.

<syntaxhighlight lang="bash">
./scripts/find_devices.sh                  # scan and report
./scripts/glowlamp.py discover             # the same thing
</syntaxhighlight>

mDNS does not cross VLANs or a guest network, so this only finds lamps on the
same subnet. An unprovisioned lamp is not on the network at all &mdash; look for
its setup access point instead.

== First-time setup ==

# Power on an unprovisioned lamp. It raises an access point named <code><hostname>-Setup-XXXXXX</code>.
# Join it; the captive portal opens.
# Pick the network, enter the password, save. The lamp reboots and joins.
# Find it with <code>./scripts/find_devices.sh</code> and open its page to set the name.

WiFi credentials are never compiled into the firmware, so a release binary is
safe to publish and any lamp can be re-provisioned in the field.

== Web interface ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Path !! What
|-
| <code>/</code> || Status, and the controls: on/off, brightness, identify
|-
| <code>/settings</code> || Firmware updates, effects and colors, names, MQTT broker, reboot
|-
| <code>/help</code> || The REST API, documented by the lamp itself
|-
| <code>/api</code> || The same reference as JSON
|-
| <code>/wifi</code> || WiFi setup pages
|}

'''Identify''' blinks the ring white for a few seconds at a floor brightness, so a
lamp dimmed to 5 in a bright room still announces itself. It overrides power and
restores whatever was showing, so identifying a lamp that is off leaves it off.

== Effects ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Effect !! What it does
|-
| <code>blend</code> || Holds on a color, then eases to the next. ''The default.''
|-
| <code>loop</code> || Walks the palette steadily, never resting on a color.
|-
| <code>flicker</code> || Similar colors mixing and guttering, like a flame. The one effect that lights the ring several colors at once.
|-
| <code>neon</code> || One color, steady, with the stutter of a failing neon tube.
|}

Up to '''5 colors'''. Colors are carried as hue and saturation rather than RGB, and
transitions travel the short way around the color wheel: an RGB blend between
two colors far apart on the wheel passes through gray and reads as dirty white
on a WS2812B.

An effect set over REST is '''temporary'''. It reverts to the default after 5
minutes unless told otherwise, is capped at 8 hours, and does not survive a
reboot &mdash; a lamp left mid-experiment finds its own way back. Anything set
over MQTT does '''not''' expire, because Home Assistant is a controller rather than
someone trying something out.

== REST API ==

Full reference: <code>docs/rest-api.md</code>, or <code>http://<lamp>/help</code> on the lamp itself.

{| class="wikitable"
! Method !! Path !! Body
|-
| GET || <code>/api/status</code> ||
|-
| POST || <code>/api/power</code> || <code>{"on": true &#124; false &#124; "toggle"}</code>
|-
| POST || <code>/api/brightness</code> || <code>{"value": 0-255}</code>
|-
| POST || <code>/api/identify</code> || <code>{"seconds": 1-60}</code>
|-
| POST || <code>/api/effect</code> || <code>{"effect": "...", "colors": [...], "seconds": 300}</code>
|-
| POST || <code>/api/effect/reset</code> ||
|-
| POST || <code>/api/name</code> || <code>{"name": "...", "hostname": "..."}</code>
|-
| POST || <code>/api/broker</code> || <code>{"host": "...", "port": 1883}</code>
|-
| POST || <code>/api/ota/check</code> ||
|-
| POST || <code>/api/ota/install</code> ||
|-
| POST || <code>/api/ota/update</code> ||
|-
| POST || <code>/api/reboot</code> ||
|}

<syntaxhighlight lang="bash">
curl http://castor-lamp.local/api/status
curl -X POST 'http://castor-lamp.local/api/power?on=false'
curl -X POST 'http://castor-lamp.local/api/brightness?value=128'
curl -X POST 'http://castor-lamp.local/api/effect?effect=neon&colors=ff0000,00ff00'
</syntaxhighlight>

'''A JSON body is only read when the request sets <code>Content-Type: application/json</code>.'''
Curl's default content type is form-encoded, and a JSON document sent that way
is discarded by the HTTP server before the firmware sees it &mdash; silently.
Query parameters never have that problem.

Anything that changes the lamp is POST only. There is no authentication: these
are LAN devices, and anything that can reach one can control it.

== MQTT ==

Full reference: <code>docs/mqtt.md</code>.

Set a broker and the lamp publishes a retained Home Assistant discovery config,
appearing as <code>light.<name></code> with brightness, a color picker and the effect
list &mdash; no YAML, no restart, no filesystem access to the Home Assistant host.

<syntaxhighlight lang="bash">
./scripts/glowlamp.py broker 192.168.1.10 --host castor-lamp.local
</syntaxhighlight>

{| class="wikitable"
! Topic !! Direction !! Retained
|-
| <code>homeassistant/light/glowlamp_<mac>/config</code> || publish || yes
|-
| <code>glowlamp/<hostname>/state</code> || publish || yes
|-
| <code>glowlamp/<hostname>/availability</code> || publish || yes
|-
| <code>glowlamp/<hostname>/set</code> || subscribe || '''never'''
|}

Commands must never be retained: a retained command replays on every reconnect
and pins the lamp to whatever was last sent.

Command payloads use Home Assistant's JSON light schema. <code>state</code> must be the
'''word''' <code>"ON"</code> or <code>"OFF"</code> &mdash; a boolean is accepted silently and never
turns the lamp on.

<syntaxhighlight lang="bash">
mosquitto_pub -h broker -t glowlamp/castor-lamp/set -m '{"state":"ON","brightness":200}'
mosquitto_pub -h broker -t glowlamp/castor-lamp/set -m '{"effect":"flicker"}'
</syntaxhighlight>

MQTT is optional and off by default. A lamp with no broker never connects and is
fully usable over REST, which stays the interface for the scripts, identify and
OTA.

